		<div id="yaanvn">
			<div class="BackgroundWhiteElement PaddingXSixElement PaddingYTwentyElement LargePaddingXEightElement">
				<div class="MarginXAutoElement MaxWidthThreeXL TextBaseElement LeadingSevenElement TextGraySevenHundredElement">
					<h1 class="MarginTopTwoElement TextThreeXL FontBoldElement TrackingTightElement TextGrayNineHundredElement SmallTextFourXL">Choosing the Right Colours for a Welcoming English Home</h1>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Colour has a quiet but powerful effect on how a home feels. The right palette can make a room seem calm, sociable or restful, while helping different spaces feel connected. Rather than choosing shades one at a time, consider how they work together and how they respond to the light and furnishings in your home.</p>
					<h3 class="MarginTopEightElement TextXL">Start with the light</h3>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">A colour can look quite different in a bright room, a shaded corner or under artificial lighting. Notice how daylight changes throughout the day, and whether the room receives direct sun. These observations can help you decide whether a shade feels comfortable in the space or appears too cool, too strong or too dark.</p>
					<figure class="MarginTopEightElement">
						<img style="margin: 0 auto" class="AspectVideoElement RoundedXL BackgroundGrayFiftyElement ObjectCoverElement" src="img/ce0abd3ebd2226eecf26e32237a86b70.jpg" alt="" />
					</figure>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Before committing, paint sample patches on more than one wall. Look at them at different times and with the room’s usual lighting switched on. If possible, view the samples alongside flooring, curtains and larger pieces of furniture. This is more reliable than choosing from a small colour card alone.</p>
					<h3 class="MarginTopEightElement TextXL">Choose a feeling for each room</h3>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Think about how you want to use a room before deciding on its colour. A bedroom may benefit from shades that feel restful, while a living room might call for a palette that feels relaxed and welcoming. In a kitchen or dining space, consider how the colours sit with worktops, cabinetry and the light at different times of day.</p>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">There is no single set of colours that suits every room. The aim is to choose shades that support the way you live, rather than follow a rule about which colours belong in particular spaces.</p>
					<h3 class="MarginTopEightElement TextXL">Build a palette, not a collection of favourites</h3>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Begin with one or two colours you are confident you want to live with. Then add supporting shades that complement them. These might be lighter or deeper versions of the same colour, or contrasting tones used in smaller amounts.</p>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">A simple palette can help a home feel cohesive without making every room look identical. Carry a colour through adjoining spaces in details such as painted woodwork, textiles or accessories. In a room that needs its own character, use a related shade that sits comfortably alongside the rest of the scheme.</p>
					<h3 class="MarginTopEightElement TextXL">Consider neutrals carefully</h3>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Neutral colours can create a versatile backdrop, but they are not all the same. Some appear warmer, while others have a cooler quality. Compare samples with the fixed elements in the room, including floors, tiles and stone, so the overall scheme feels balanced.</p>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">A neutral room can still feel inviting through variation in texture and tone. Upholstery, rugs, curtains and natural materials can add depth without introducing many competing colours.</p>
					<h3 class="MarginTopEightElement TextXL">Use stronger colours with intention</h3>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Deeper or more vivid shades can bring definition to a room. If you are unsure about using them across every wall, try them on a smaller area, such as a door, alcove or piece of furniture. This lets you see how the colour behaves in the space and how it works with the wider palette.</p>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">The balance matters as much as the shade itself. Consider how much of the room the colour will cover, what surrounds it and whether it is repeated elsewhere. A strong colour used thoughtfully can feel welcoming rather than overwhelming.</p>
					<h3 class="MarginTopEightElement TextXL">Check the whole scheme before painting</h3>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Bring samples together with the materials and furnishings that will remain in the room. Consider the view from nearby spaces, especially where doorways connect rooms or where sightlines are open. A colour that works well on its own may feel less harmonious when seen alongside another room.</p>
					<p class="MarginTopEightElement">Take time to live with your choices before making a final decision. Colour samples, changing light and existing finishes all influence the result. A considered approach helps create a home that feels personal, comfortable and connected from room to room.</p>
